Receipt IV expanded estimation, version 1.1.0
Revised October 3 UTC / October 2 Toronto, 2026

The full calendar-complete cohort is now the primary receipt-IV sample. The
November 2025 color reform no longer imposes the primary censor. The pre-color
estimate and January 30 source-day guard remain sensitivity fits. This replaces
the primary-design recommendation in version 1.0.0.

Sample                                      Exposures
Primary: follow-up ends before May 1, 2026    3,917,764
Pre-color guard: before Nov 18, 07:15 UTC     3,080,218
Mechanics guard: before Jan 30, 00:00 UTC     3,449,729
Common >=10 prior opponent decisions         3,253,387

Each endpoint is exclusive: anchor_ms + 90 days < endpoint. The primary retains
2,233,890 reached cases and 1,683,874 complete-window nonreachers. Nonreachers
keep Y=0. Both __PRIVATE_VALUE_1__ and __PRIVATE_VALUE_2__ remain included.

The primary is a pooled estimate across policy regimes. May 1 marks calendar
coverage, not policy stability. January 30 is a source-informed conservative
guard, not an established deployment date. Changes in receipt, opportunity
composition or response can affect interpretation and instrument weighting.

Run

Save Receipt_IV_Expanded_Estimation_v1_1_0.zip in ~/Desktop/Lichess_Desktop.
Extract it there, then run in Terminal:

  bash "$HOME/Desktop/Lichess_Desktop/Receipt_IV_Expanded_Estimation_v1_1_0/RUN_RECEIPT_IV_EXPANDED_ESTIMATION.command"

Use this package in place of the pre-color v1.0.0 launcher. It reads the saved
enriched private Parquets and historical cohort on XT_Pro. It makes no API
requests and does not redo source recovery, rating replay, cohort assembly,
scores, state attachment or Stockfish.

The launcher uses /Volumes/XT_Pro/lichess_kindness/venv/bin/python, prevents Mac
sleep, and allows up to ten DuckDB threads. Fits run sequentially to limit
memory. There are no scheduled pauses or prompts. Heartbeats continue every
30 seconds. Private-sample runtime has not been benchmarked.

Requirements: the existing NumPy, PyArrow and DuckDB environment, mounted XT_Pro,
and at least 8 GiB free working disk space. Nothing is installed or upgraded.
The 8 GiB memory flag is a DuckDB/planning budget, not an OS-enforced NumPy limit.

What changed

The primary adds exposure month x assigned index chooser color fixed effects
to the original assignment-cell, exposure-date and hour-of-week fixed effects
and predetermined controls. calendar90_legacy_controls uses the identical full
rows with the original controls, isolating that addition.

Realized later-choice color, timing, policy period and reach are neither
controls nor selection rules. Their metadata are audited and summarized.
All ten later choices in the seven-minute implementation bracket remain.
No later-choice color is invented for nonreachers.

Color-window diagnostics use only the index anchor and fixed 90-day horizon:
before = the horizon ends before Nov 18, 07:15 UTC; after = the anchor is on/after
Nov 18, 07:22 UTC; spanning = every remaining primary window. Counts are
3,080,218 / 379,110 / 458,436, respectively. Contrasts are calendar diagnostics,
not causal reform effects or independent tests. Month x index-color adjustment
does not remove all later-choice policy effects or certify exclusion.

Fifteen specifications are frozen in DESIGN_FREEZE.json:

  1. Exact historical reproduction using original controls.
  2. Full calendar primary with month x assigned index-color adjustment.
  3. Identical full rows with original controls.
  4. Common >=10-history subset.
  5-6. Lag-7 and lag-30 instruments on identical lag-common rows.
  7. Identical primary rows without recipient-history controls.
  8-9. Repeat-recipient pooled and recipient-FE diagnostic on common rows.
  10. Pre-color guard.
  11. January 30 mechanics guard.
  12-13. Color-spanning and post-color calendar windows.
  14-15. Old-calendar/new-history and later-calendar extension.

Five and ten are eligibility floors for the same saved all-history score.
Both common-sample labels reference one fit; no last-five/last-ten instrument
is invented. Lag comparisons change the instrument and corresponding opponent
history controls on common rows; recipient-history windows stay fixed.
Sensitivity subsets follow the saved spacing, without re-spacing.

Verification and interpretation

The program authenticates the state-diagnostics receipt chain and historical
cohort SHA. Before any new coefficient is fitted, it must reproduce the
historical IV coefficient, SE, RF and first stage. Rounded benchmarks and
production counts are fixed; there is no CLI bypass.

Historical/new overlap uses game and recipient keys, not row numbers. Common
outcomes and predetermined fields must agree. The full N is 46.06% larger than
historical N=2,682,330: a net size comparison, not an append-only claim or a
precision gain. The bridge reports common/new-only/old-only membership and
changes in historical scores explicitly.

Original covariance and null-restricted AR routines are byte-identical vendored
source. Inference uses shared-account OR exposure-date CR0 dependence, treating
historical scores as fixed. Read first stage, RF, 2SLS, AR sets, balance and
exclusion sensitivity together. Date counts and unsupported fits remain
visible; covariance matrices are not repaired.

Direct-effect sensitivity projects AR acceptance over fixed gamma ranges
analytically, retaining first-stage uncertainty. Tipping values are recomputed.
Gamma and instrument effects use reference SD 0.059411690344000595. JSON retains
unbounded/disconnected sets. The ordinary-game placebo is not subtracted.
MDE80 is a descriptive normal approximation from fitted SEs, not prospective
power or a behavioral GO decision.

Expanded prospective-entry and ordinary-game placebo estimates and mechanics
deployment timing remain unresolved. Current-event selection and exclusion
remain assumptions. Rating-payoff calibration and fractional-display visibility
belong to the separate color-incentive study; they are not prerequisites for
fitting this historical-opponent-leniency receipt IV.

Outputs and restart

- Log, aggregate result ZIP and SHA sidecar: ~/Desktop/Lichess_Desktop.
- Private root: /Volumes/XT_Pro/lichess_kindness/derived/replication_36m/receipt_iv_expanded_estimation_v110_PRIVATE
- Public aggregates: /Volumes/XT_Pro/lichess_kindness/output/receipt_iv_expanded_estimation_v110
- Primary: ESTIMATES.json, calendar90_primary, estimator 2SLS.
- Compare calendar90_legacy_controls, guard_precolor90 and guard_mechanics90.
- CALENDAR_REGIME_COUNTS reports fixed-window counts by assigned color.
- LATER_CHOICE_POLICY_DESCRIPTIVE describes realized choices only.

Return the result ZIP, .sha256 sidecar and log for review. Raw game/account rows
stay private; a positive allowlist controls exports. After interruption, run the
same command. It verifies inputs and reuses completed stages, retaining partial
directories. A lock prevents concurrent copies of this version. Earlier v1.0.0
artifacts remain untouched; do not run both versions concurrently.

All 15 synthetic tests passed, including the full fifteen-specification path,
cached restart, complete nonreachers, policy boundaries, original-estimator
identity, independent dense-regression verification of the added fixed effects,
dependence covariance, and bounded-gamma AR inference. Thin synthetic subsamples
are explicitly marked unsupported. These tests do not replace the production
reproduction gate or execution on the Mac. No new production coefficient is
claimed here.
